<Base title="About" description="Electrical engineer, 30+ years writing software from embedded firmware to healthcare systems, now focused on AI-assisted development.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    I'm an electrical engineer by training (BSEE, University of Pittsburgh) and I've been
    writing software since 1990. I've worked across nearly every layer of the stack, and AI
    is the biggest change to how software gets built that I've seen.
  </p>

  <p>
    I started in embedded systems at AMETEK: assembly-language firmware, custom circuit
    boards, and real-time signal processing for analytical instruments. I was also putting
    instrument control on the web well before that was common. As a contract engineer at
    McNair Software I wrote firmware for FDA-regulated home dialysis equipment, and I built a
    lab automation system that cut manual testing time by 60%.
  </p>

  <p>
    From there, healthcare became the through-line. I built pharmacy informatics tools at
    Keystone Therapeutics, including clinical decision support, antimicrobial stewardship,
    and training simulators. One of those products, Doseresponse, was acquired by MedKeeper /
    Becton Dickinson. I then spent fifteen years there as a Staff Software Engineer, building
    HIPAA-compliant systems used by hospital networks and major health insurers.
  </p>

  <p>
    For more than a decade I've also volunteered as CIO of a nonprofit that connects new
    mothers with experienced mentors by text message. I built its secure messaging platform
    and I'm now leading its modernization.
  </p>

  <p>
    Today I work independently. My focus is helping teams adopt AI development tools and
    build AI into their products, using the same care about correctness that regulated
    software demands. I write my process down and publish it, because I think the discipline
    matters more than the tools.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm based in Western Pennsylvania, north of Pittsburgh, and work with clients anywhere,
    remotely.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
  </div>
</Base>
